  "worked_solution": "We shall use the following identity:\n\n$$\n\u2207\\times (f\\,F)=f\u2207\\times F+\u2207f\\times F\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $\u2207f\\times F.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f\\times F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2{e}^{2x} & 0 & 0 \\\\ z & 0 & \\operatorname{ln}\u2061x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[0(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x)-0(0)]i-[2{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x)-0(z)]j+[2{e}^{2x}(0)-0(z)]k \\\\  & =0\\,i-2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x\\,j+0\\,k \\\\  & =\u27e80,-2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x,0\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207\\times (f\\,F) & =f\u2207\\times F+\u2207f\\times F \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\u27e80,1-\\frac{1}{x},0\u27e9+\u27e80,-2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x,0\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e80,{e}^{2x}-\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{x},0\u27e9+\u27e80,-2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x,0\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e80,{e}^{2x}-\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{x}-2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x,0\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e80,{e}^{2x}(1-\\frac{1}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x),0\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207\\times (f\\,F)(1,1,-4) & =\u27e80,{e}^{2(1)}(1-\\frac{1}{(1)}-2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1)),0\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e80,0,0\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
